```java
// Interface: pure contract, no shared code
interface Notifier {
    void send(String message);
}

// Abstract class: shared implementation, one hook left abstract
abstract class BaseNotifier {
    void log(String message) { System.out.println("Sending: " + message); }
    void send(String message) {
        log(message);
        deliver(message);
    }
    abstract void deliver(String message);
}
```

Both let `EmailNotifier` and `SmsNotifier` implement `send()`. The interface leaves everything to each implementer; the abstract class hands them shared logging and a template method, at the cost of taking their one supertype slot.

```java
interface Notifier {
    void send(String message);
}

abstract class BaseNotifier implements Notifier {
    protected void log(String message) { System.out.println("Sending: " + message); }
    public void send(String message) {
        log(message);
        deliver(message);
    }
    protected abstract void deliver(String message);
}

class EmailNotifier extends BaseNotifier {
    protected void deliver(String message) { /* SMTP call */ }
}
```

This combined shape is right when you want the *public contract* (`Notifier`) to stay implementable by anyone, while still giving your own family of implementers (`BaseNotifier`'s subclasses) shared logging and a template method for free.
